# Git Origin vs Upstream (Interview + Practical Notes)

| **Origin**                                                    | **Upstream**                                                             |
| ------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Refers to **your own remote repository** (usually on GitHub). | Refers to the **original repository** from which you forked the project. |
| Created automatically when you clone a repository.            | Added manually after forking a repository.                               |
| Used to push your own changes.                                | Used to fetch updates from the original project.                         |

---

## 1. What is `origin`?

`origin` is the **default name** Git gives to the remote repository when you clone a project.

### Example

```bash
git clone https://github.com/sameer/demo.git
```

Git automatically creates:

```text
origin → https://github.com/sameer/demo.git
```

### Industry Use Case

You developed a new feature and want to upload your code to your GitHub repository.

```bash
git push origin feature-login
```

---

## 2. What is `upstream`?

`upstream` is the **original repository** from which you forked a project.

It is commonly used in **open-source development**.

### Example

Original repository:

```text
https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes
```

Your fork:

```text
https://github.com/sameer/kubernetes
```

Remote configuration:

```text
origin   → Your fork
upstream → Original Kubernetes repository
```

---

## Industry Use Case

Suppose you fork an open-source project.

You:

1. Fork the repository.
2. Clone **your fork**.
3. Add the original repository as **upstream**.
4. Regularly fetch updates from **upstream**.
5. Push your work to **origin**.
6. Create a Pull Request to contribute changes back to the original project.

Commands:

```bash
git remote add upstream https://github.com/original/project.git
git fetch upstream
git merge upstream/main
git push origin main
```

